 "Why a Behavior Tree here and a State Tree there": "abyss/tdd/",
 "Behavior Tree and State Tree comparison": "abyss/tdd/",
 "Behavior Tree": "abyss/tdd/",
 "Model": "abyss/tdd/",
 "Priority tree, re-evaluated continuously": "abyss/tdd/",
 "Hierarchical state machine with explicit transitions": "abyss/tdd/",
 "Strengths": "abyss/tdd/",
 "Reactive (decorators, observer aborts), mature tooling, Gameplay Debugger support": "abyss/tdd/",
 "The current state is explicit and readable; runs on any Actor through a component, without an AI Controller": "abyss/tdd/",
 "Weaknesses": "abyss/tdd/",
 "States are implicit; the Blackboard is rigid": "abyss/tdd/",
 "Younger system, API still evolving between engine versions": "abyss/tdd/",
 "<span class=\"n\">→</span>Decision": "abyss/tdd/",
 "Sea serpent": "abyss/tdd/",
 "<mark class=\"hl\">Behavior Tree.</mark> A reactive, priority-driven brain: being deterred must interrupt a charge. Observer aborts express this directly.": "abyss/tdd/",
 "Shoal": "abyss/tdd/",
 "<mark class=\"hl\">State Tree.</mark> A group with a few clear states (Cruise, Flee, Regroup), and a single actor that is not a pawn. A State Tree component fits it without a fake AI Controller.": "abyss/tdd/",
 "Rule": "abyss/tdd/",
 "<mark class=\"hl\">One brain per creature.</mark> Mixing both on the same creature would make “who decides?” ambiguous and harder to debug.": "abyss/tdd/",
